  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Both sides are positive for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so take logarithms to base 2. The power law gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> Put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Recovering the original variable gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Their product is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, so choose D. Each positive candidate satisfies the original equation. Equivalently, the sum of the logarithmic roots is 3, so their original values have product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>"
